{
  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on the presence of a sure outcome (i.e., an outcome that occurs with 100% probability) in each lottery. A sure outcome is one that the decision maker will receive regardless of uncertainty. In Lottery A, the outcomes are 6000 Euros with 25% probability and 0 Euros with 75% — no outcome occurs with certainty. In Lottery B, the outcomes are 4000 with 25%, 2000 with 25%, and 0 with 50% — again, no outcome occurs with 100% probability. Therefore, neither lottery contains a sure outcome. The decision reason cannot apply because it requires at least one lottery to have a sure outcome to express a preference. The relevant information derived is: no sure outcome exists in either lottery, so the reason would be indifferent or unable to make a choice.",
