  "step_3": "The decision reason depends on identifying a sure (certain) outcome in either lottery and preferring the one that has it. However, neither lottery has a sure outcome, so the reason cannot be applied meaningfully. The individual, meanwhile, is considering the probability of winning anything (non-zero outcomes) and prefers Lottery B because it offers a higher chance (50% vs 25%) of winning some positive amount. This reasoning is related to the 'chance of winning something' but not to the presence of a sure outcome. The information considered by the individual (non-zero win probability) is different from the information required by the decision reason (presence of 100% outcome). The justification (favoring higher chance of any win) does not align with the rule of the decision reason (favoring a sure outcome). Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual used this decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

